INT. BOWLING ALLEY - LANE 12 - FINAL FRAME - NIGHT

Neon pins. Sticky carpet. A trophy case. The league championship banner: DRY CREEK LANES - TUESDAY NIGHT FINALS. A crowd packs the seats behind the rail, sweating, silent.

COACH MURIEL TAPIA, 60s, windbreaker, clipboard, paces behind the scorers' table. Next to her, DESHAWN "THE HAMMER" PRITCHETT, 30s, in a team polo, chalks his fingers, rolling his shoulders. At the lane, ANNIKA BERGSTROM-ODUYA, 20s, ponytail, holds her ball against her chest. Opposite them on the other bench, rival captain HANK WILLOUGHBY, 50s, red bowling shirt, smirks and cracks his knuckles. At the scoring console, young scorekeeper PRESTON LI, 17, headset on, watches the monitor.

MURIEL
Ten pins. That's all she needs.

DESHAWN
She's got ice in her veins.

MURIEL
She's got a ball in her hands. Pray it's the right one.

Annika steps up to the approach. Breathes. Stares at the head pin. The crowd hushes. Hank leans forward, grinning. Preston grips his headset.

ANNIKA
(under her breath)
Spare. Just spare.

She takes four steps. Swings. Releases. The ball tears down the lane, hooks left, a streak of black. It slams the pocket. Pins scatter. Nine fall. One wobbles. Stands.

The crowd groans. Hank punches the air.

HANK
Told you she'd choke!

DESHAWN
Sit down, Hank.

HANK
Make me.

Muriel puts a hand on Deshawn's chest. He holds. Annika walks back, staring at the single pin.

PRESTON (V.O.)
(over the PA, shaky)
Attention, bowlers. Lane twelve. Tenth frame. One ball remaining. Annika Bergstrom-Oduya needs the spare for the title.

MURIEL
(low)
Annie. Look at me.

ANNIKA
I'm looking.

MURIEL
That pin has been waiting thirty years for someone to knock it down.

ANNIKA
It's been waiting for me.

MURIEL
It's been waiting for all of us.

Deshawn offers a towel. She wipes her hands. Hands it back. Steps up again. The crowd leans in. Hank folds his arms, jaw tight.

She sets her feet. Looks at the pin. Takes the four steps again. Releases.

The ball rolls slower this time. Gentle. A curve so slight it barely bends. It kisses the last pin at the edge. The pin spins. Tilts. Falls.

For one second, nothing. Then the whole alley erupts. Muriel throws her clipboard in the air. Deshawn lifts Annika off the floor. Preston screams into his headset. Hank sits with his arms still folded.

MURIEL (CONT'D)
That's a title!

DESHAWN
That's a Tuesday!

Annika laughs and cries at once, still held aloft. Hank rises, walks over, offers a hand across the rail.

HANK
Good game.

ANNIKA
(shaking it)
Thanks, Hank.

HANK
Next year, I'm bringing the red ball.

ANNIKA
Bring two.
